WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.633
A practical starting pattern is one front end with multiple inference nodes.

00:00:04.633 --> 00:00:08.410
The front end provides a stable user or application interface,

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:11.054
while each worker hosts a model server.

00:00:11.054 --> 00:00:17.097
A router or load balancer sends requests only to workers capable of serving the requested model.

00:00:17.097 --> 00:00:21.629
Replicating the same model across several nodes increases concurrent capacity and creates

00:00:21.629 --> 00:00:23.518
options for maintenance or failure,

00:00:23.518 --> 00:00:26.540
provided the routing layer removes unhealthy instances promptly.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.806
Another pattern assigns separate model roles.

00:00:28.806 --> 00:00:33.338
One node may serve a general language model, another a coding model,

00:00:33.338 --> 00:00:38.626
and a smaller CPU or GPU service may generate embeddings or rerank search results.

00:00:38.626 --> 00:00:44.292
Role separation makes resource use predictable and prevents a large model from evicting smaller services.

00:00:44.292 --> 00:00:48.825
CPU support nodes can host APIs, queues, databases, observability, and retrieval components,

00:00:48.825 --> 00:00:51.091
preserving expensive GPU capacity for inference.

00:00:51.091 --> 00:00:53.735
Storage and management may also be separated.

00:00:53.735 --> 00:00:59.400
A storage node can hold controlled model versions and distribute them to local worker caches.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.555
A management node can provide configuration, monitoring, secrets, and deployment coordination.

00:01:03.555 --> 00:01:07.332
Avoid making either service an undocumented single point of failure.

00:01:07.332 --> 00:01:11.109
Define what happens when shared storage, the router, name resolution,

00:01:11.109 --> 00:01:13.376
or the management plane is unavailable.

00:01:13.376 --> 00:01:16.020
Local clusters favor privacy and predictable ownership.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.041
Cloud clusters favor rapid provisioning and elastic capacity.

00:01:19.041 --> 00:01:20.930
Hybrid designs can combine them,

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:25.085
but data classification and routing policy must make the boundary explicit.

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:28.484
Start with the fewest roles that meet the requirement.

00:01:28.484 --> 00:01:29.617
Add replication, queues,

00:01:29.617 --> 00:01:33.772
and management services in response to observed failure or capacity needs,

00:01:33.772 --> 00:01:38.305
because every architectural box becomes software that must be secured, monitored, upgraded,

00:01:38.305 --> 00:01:39.060
and recovered.
